 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting takes $O(N \log N)$ on the CPU [S5, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(N)$ for the draw call, but fragment shading cost depends on the number of overlapping splats per pixel.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing Gaussian data and sorted indices [S1, S2, unofficial]. VRAM usage for textures is $\approx 28$ bytes/splat for SH0, totaling $\approx 14$ MB for 500k splats [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** CPU radix sort of ~500k depth keys takes a few milliseconds on a worker thread [S5, unofficial]. Sorting 8k splats in JS takes ~1 ms [S5, unofficial]. Mobile-GS reports up to 127 FPS on modern mobile GPUs using this instanced approach with first-order SH [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; works on hardware without compute shaders (e.g., older WebGL, Filament 1.71.5) [S5, unofficial]; stable visual output with correct alpha blending [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Less efficient than tile-based GPU rasterization for large scenes due to CPU sorting overhead and lack of early ray termination [S1, S5, unofficial]; limited by 16-bit index buffer (65535 instances per draw call) requiring batching [S5, unofficial]; SH degree limited to 0 or 1 on mobile to stay within VRAM [S5, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Display-only visualization, web viewers, mobile apps with limited GPU compute support, or when simplicity is prioritized over maximum performance [S5, S6, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Real-time training, very large scenes (>1M splats) on low-end hardware, or when order-independent transparency is required without sorting [S1, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting dominates the CPU cost. CPU radix sort of ~500k depth keys takes a few milliseconds on a worker thread [S5, unofficial]. JS native sort takes ~1 ms for 8k splats [S5, unofficial]. Re-sorting is gated on camera movement, not every frame [S5, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing splat data. SH0 splats require ~28 bytes/splat in RGBA16F textures (~14 MB VRAM for 500k splats) [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Mobile-GS reports up to 127 FPS on modern mobile GPUs using first-order SH and OIT [S5, unofficial]. However, CPU sorting is less efficient than the official 3DGS renderer's tile-based CUDA rasterizer [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, works on hardware without compute shaders (e.g., older WebGL, Filament 1.71.5) [S5, unofficial], compatible with standard alpha blending pipelines.
+*   **Limitations:** CPU sorting becomes a bottleneck for very large scenes (>1M splats) [S1, unofficial]. Limited to SH0 or SH1 on mid-range mobile to stay within VRAM budgets [S5, unofficial]. Instance count limited to 65,535 per draw call, requiring batching [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Display-only visualization on Android, iOS, Web, and ROS RViz [S2, S5, S6, unofficial]. When GPU compute shaders are unavailable or undesirable.
+*   **When not to use:** Real-time interactive rendering of massive scenes (>1M splats) where GPU sorting is required [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method is based on the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial]. It was popularized by the "3D Gaussian Splatting in a Weekend" tutorial [S1, unofficial] and implemented in various open-source projects such as RVizSplat [S2, unofficial], Gaussian-Splatting-WebViewers [S3, unofficial], and Splaty [S4, unofficial]. It is used in Android SplatNode, Filament-based renderers, and web viewers [S5, S6, unofficial].
+This method is based on the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial]. It was developed as a display-only renderer for trained 3DGS scenes, initially popularized by the "3D Gaussian Splatting in a Weekend" tutorial [S1, unofficial]. It has been implemented in various contexts, including RViz for ROS [S2, unofficial], web viewers [S3, unofficial], Android/iOS via Filament [S5, unofficial], and Unity [S6, unofficial]. The approach was chosen for its simplicity and compatibility with standard graphics APIs when GPU compute shaders were not available or practical [S5, unofficial].
 
